WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.460 --> 00:00:03.740
Duct tape can hold

00:00:03.740 --> 00:00:05.680
just about anything together.

00:00:05.690 --> 00:00:07.900
We use it for all sorts of repairs

00:00:07.900 --> 00:00:09.730
and even as a decorative addition

00:00:09.730 --> 00:00:11.860
to various arts and crafts.

00:00:11.860 --> 00:00:15.823
But what makes duct tape
so strong and versatile?

00:00:17.130 --> 00:00:19.773
And is it duct tape or duck tape?

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:23.540
The tape was originally called duck tape

00:00:23.540 --> 00:00:25.763
because of its water-resistant qualities.

00:00:25.994 --> 00:00:28.136
It repelled water like a duck's back,

00:00:28.152 --> 00:00:30.587
and it used to be made out
of a cotton duck fabric

00:00:30.603 --> 00:00:33.320
without any adhesive layers.

00:00:33.320 --> 00:00:37.231
But cotton duck fabric doesn't
actually come from ducks.

00:00:37.231 --> 00:00:38.071
(squeak)

00:00:38.096 --> 00:00:39.710
This is what it looks like.

00:00:39.801 --> 00:00:42.116
It's a strong fabric made from cotton,

00:00:42.166 --> 00:00:45.107
where the threads make
a crisscross pattern.

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:48.670
Known for being a strong
material, the fabric was used

00:00:48.670 --> 00:00:52.040
to protect power cables
and electrical conductors

00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:52.873
from corrosion.

00:00:53.373 --> 00:00:56.914
But the adhesive duct tape
we know and love today

00:00:56.943 --> 00:00:59.448
didn't appear until World War II.

00:00:59.498 --> 00:01:02.137
A factory worker who
was packaging ammunition

00:01:02.137 --> 00:01:06.391
sealed each box with tape and
wax to make them waterproof.

00:01:06.690 --> 00:01:10.400
When the worker, Vesta Stout,
saw soldiers struggling

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:13.210
to open the boxes, she
came up with an idea

00:01:13.210 --> 00:01:17.700
to seal the boxes with a strong
cloth-based waterproof tape.

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:21.330
Stout wrote a letter to
FDR about her solution

00:01:21.330 --> 00:01:23.740
and, a few weeks later, received word

00:01:23.740 --> 00:01:25.380
from the War Production Board

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:28.770
that Johnson and Johnson would
be manufacturing the tape.

00:01:28.770 --> 00:01:31.193
The tape became a military sensation.

00:01:31.731 --> 00:01:34.732
It was durable and easy to
apply and remove by hand.

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:38.620
After the war ended, duct tape
turned up in hardware stores

00:01:38.620 --> 00:01:41.930
ready to help Americans
with household repairs, too.

00:01:41.930 --> 00:01:45.470
It quickly became a useful
tool for wrapping air ducts,

00:01:45.470 --> 00:01:48.230
which led to its other name, duct tape.

00:01:48.230 --> 00:01:52.620
Duct tape used to be kind of
a one-and-done silver tape

00:01:52.620 --> 00:01:54.740
that was essentially used for repairs.

00:01:54.740 --> 00:01:57.730
Our adhesive is specifically
formulated to stick to

00:01:57.730 --> 00:02:01.340
a whole variety of different
surfaces, and then basically

00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:04.320
the waterproof backing on
top of that then protects

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:07.320
the adhesive once it's
applied to a surface.

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:10.069
Narrator: There are three main components

00:02:10.069 --> 00:02:11.556
that go into making the tape.

00:02:11.556 --> 00:02:13.760
Rubber for the adhesive,
cloth, and backing.

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:15.830
The rubber comes from rubber trees

00:02:15.830 --> 00:02:18.630
and arrives at the factory in large bales.

00:02:18.630 --> 00:02:21.220
A machine mixes the raw rubber along

00:02:21.220 --> 00:02:23.660
with various sticky
resins until it reaches

00:02:23.660 --> 00:02:25.790
the consistency of pizza dough.

00:02:25.790 --> 00:02:30.500
Then, the mixture is heated to
over 200 degrees Fahrenheit.

00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:32.990
Now the adhesive is ready to be added

00:02:32.990 --> 00:02:34.810
to the cloth and the backing.

00:02:34.810 --> 00:02:38.130
The cloth, sometimes called
the scrim, lies in between

00:02:38.130 --> 00:02:41.810
the sticky rubber layer and
that ubiquitous silver backing.

00:02:41.810 --> 00:02:45.220
It's made from cotton and
is a key part of the tape.

00:02:45.220 --> 00:02:48.260
The cloth is what gives the
tape its tensile strength.

00:02:48.260 --> 00:02:51.663
It makes it easy to hand
tear and keeps the tape stuck

00:02:51.664 --> 00:02:54.354
to a surface once it's laid down.

00:02:54.358 --> 00:02:56.028
Finally, there's the backing.

00:02:56.061 --> 00:02:58.165
The backing is made from polyethylene,

00:02:58.290 --> 00:03:01.020
the same plastic material
used to make bags

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:02.360
and shampoo bottles.

00:03:02.360 --> 00:03:03.960
Once the backing is ready,

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:06.240
all of the materials are put together.

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:08.170
So there's basically two ways

00:03:08.170 --> 00:03:11.150
that the backing can be
incorporated into the tape:

00:03:11.150 --> 00:03:13.740
either laminated or co-extrusion.

00:03:13.740 --> 00:03:18.349
The co-extrusion basically
melds together the backing,

00:03:18.424 --> 00:03:20.330
the cloth, and then the adhesive.

00:03:20.330 --> 00:03:23.780
So it gives you a stronger
tape that won't delaminate.

00:03:23.780 --> 00:03:27.370
It's gonna be really good in
different weather applications.

00:03:27.370 --> 00:03:29.160
Narrator: Once the layers are combined,

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:31.990
the strength of the cloth
and the plastic-like nature

00:03:31.990 --> 00:03:35.210
of the backing combine to
give the tape the strength

00:03:35.210 --> 00:03:37.550
and durability we know and love.

00:03:37.550 --> 00:03:42.260
The tape comes out in one jumbo
roll that weighs over a ton.

00:03:42.260 --> 00:03:46.120
Each giant roll can produce
over 30,000 small rolls.

00:03:46.120 --> 00:03:49.730
To get it to a smaller size,
the roll is sliced into strips.

00:03:49.730 --> 00:03:51.710
Then, the smaller tape strips

00:03:51.710 --> 00:03:54.370
are put into cores and rerolled.

00:03:54.370 --> 00:03:57.870
Finally, the tapes are placed
in their final packaging,

00:03:57.870 --> 00:03:59.520
sent down the conveyor belt,

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:01.960
and put into boxes for shipment.

00:04:01.960 --> 00:04:06.250
Since its debut, duct tape
has exploded in popularity.

00:04:06.250 --> 00:04:10.400
Today, we use it to make
flowers, bags, wallets,

00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:13.640
science projects, and various DIY crafts.

00:04:13.640 --> 00:04:17.270
Unlike your masking tapes,
scotch tapes, painter's tape,

00:04:17.270 --> 00:04:21.560
and other single-use tapes,
duct tape has a long history

00:04:21.560 --> 00:04:24.260
of being used for just about anything.

00:04:24.260 --> 00:04:27.400
By simply putting fabric
and adhesive together,

00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:30.670
Vesta Stout may have created
one of the most useful

00:04:30.670 --> 00:04:34.106
and strongest inventions we have today.

